{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 470,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get started, just letting you know\nwe have some limited time only Solar Eclipse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4810,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "merch for sale at the merch store. I’ll\ntell you know more at the end of the episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10240,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space seems pretty fundamental. To build a universe,\nsurely you need something to build it on or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16150,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in. Many, maybe most physicists now think\nthat the fabric of space emerges from something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22660,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deeper. And perhaps the most existentially\ndisturbing such proposal is that our 3-D universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27990,
    "dDurationMs": 6329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is just the inward projection of an infinitely\ndistant boundary. A hologram, or sorts. Let’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34319,
    "dDurationMs": 4885,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see how that can actually work, and what the\nholographic principle really says about the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39204,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“realness” of this universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47334,
    "dDurationMs": 3776,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You could argue that the job of physics is\nto drill down and find the most fundamental"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51110,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elements of reality. The most basic building\nblocks—the “ontological primitives”--from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56700,
    "dDurationMs": 6699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which everything else emerges. A little while\nago we asked whether the fabric of space deserved"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63399,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that status. Isaac Newton certainly thought\nso, but many pretty smart people have wondered"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69369,
    "dDurationMs": 3461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if space might actually be an emergent phenomenon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72830,
    "dDurationMs": 6570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve even seen some examples of this in\npast episodes—from the early ideas of Leibnitz"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79400,
    "dDurationMs": 6570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to modern loop quantum gravity. We’ve also\nseen how at least one dimension of space can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85970,
    "dDurationMs": 5771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emerge as the inward projection from its infinitely\ndistant boundary. This is the holographic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91741,
    "dDurationMs": 6338,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "principle—which we spent many episodes building\ntowards back in the day—culminating in a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98079,
    "dDurationMs": 5731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty intense coverage of this wild idea.\nWell, today we’re travelling back to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103810,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holographic boundary to try to understand\nbetter how the fabric of space can emerge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108240,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this way. This will set us up to do a couple\nof things in upcoming episodes: we’ll be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112830,
    "dDurationMs": 6429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "able to understand how gravity can emerge\nfrom entropic forces on a holographic boundary,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119259,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and how the fabric of space may be knit together\nby the quantum entanglement of entities that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125329,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "themselves don’t exist in our familiar spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128879,
    "dDurationMs": 4531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Feel free to watch the old holographic principle\nstuff later if you haven’t. I’m going"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133410,
    "dDurationMs": 6670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to summarize the core stuff for your convenience.\nSo the whole argument started in a surprising"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "place—with Steven Hawking and Jacob Bekenstein\nthinking about the thermodynamics of black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145360,
    "dDurationMs": 5099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes. Normally we think of thermodynamics\nas the study of the statistical behavior of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150459,
    "dDurationMs": 6170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many particles. It’s the study of emergent\nproperties like temperature and pressure of,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156629,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say, a room full of air molecules. These are\nproperties that the individual particles do"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161569,
    "dDurationMs": 1211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not have."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162780,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another key thermodynamic property is entropy.\nWe can think of this as a measure of the amount"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168080,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of hidden information in a system—information\nabout the stuff that makes up the system that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173310,
    "dDurationMs": 6789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "isn’t reflected in the observable, macroscopic\nproperties. This ro-om full of air molecules"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180099,
    "dDurationMs": 5901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has a high entropy because all of the particles’\nlocations and speeds are hidden behind just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186000,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a few thermodynamic properties that I can\nsense like the temperature and pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190530,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We wouldn’t necessarily expect an individual\nblack hole to have thermodynamic properties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196330,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, black holes are practically particle-like.\nThey are the result of the ultimate gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201720,
    "dDurationMs": 4909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse of matter and are surrounded b y\nan event horizon which prevents anything exiting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206629,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the black hole from the inside. That includes\nany information about what exactly collapsed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212129,
    "dDurationMs": 6231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to form it. Jacob Bekenstein realized that\nblack holes viewed from the exterior possess"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218360,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "three properties—mass, charge, and spin—almost\nlike an elementary particle. But it’s that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223500,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exact fact that gets us to black hole thermodynamics,\nand to emergent space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229620,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The difference between a black hole and an\nelementary particle is that the former is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233860,
    "dDurationMs": 5719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made by the extreme clumping together of the\nlatter. That means we can define the entropy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239579,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a black hole—and the number is huge because\nblack holes hide almost all of the information"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245239,
    "dDurationMs": 4901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the stuff that fell in. Bekenstein and\nHawking figured out the entropy of black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250140,
    "dDurationMs": 5269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They found that there’s around one bit of\nhidden information per 4 square Planck length units"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255409,
    "dDurationMs": 1330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256739,
    "dDurationMs": 5531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This simple formula was really surprising—and\nthat surprise led physicists down the path"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262270,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the holographic principle. See it’s actually\nquite weird that the entropy should be proportional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267210,
    "dDurationMs": 4519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the surface area. Intuitively we might\nexpect the amount of information that can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271729,
    "dDurationMs": 5991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be hidden in any region of space is proportional\nto its volume—like, one bit per tiny block"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277720,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in that volume, rather than a bit per tiny\nsquare on its surface. In fact, black holes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283070,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "represent the maximum information density\nof any volume of space—this is the Bekenstein"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289580,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bound. And that applies to the whole universe—the\ninformation about every particle in its 3-D"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296110,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "volume can, in principle, be encoded on its\n2-D surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300750,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s one thing to say that all the information\ndescribing a universe fits on its surface,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306060,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the holographic principle goes way further.\nIt suggests that we can think of our universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312440,
    "dDurationMs": 7500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as really playing out on that surface. More\nprecisely, that surface is its own lower-dimensional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319940,
    "dDurationMs": 5671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime with its own particles and fields\nand a different set of laws of physics. But"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325611,
    "dDurationMs": 6239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "encoded in the patterns that play out on that\nlower-dimensional spacetime is an entire universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331850,
    "dDurationMs": 4629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one dimension higher. It’s as though the\nvolume of the universe—what we call the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336479,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“bulk”--emerges from the dynamics on its\nsurface—the boundary."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341009,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The journey from black hole entropy to the\nHolographic Principle was led by Gerard t’Hooft"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346199,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Leonard Susskind. But it was Juan Maldacena\nwho gave us the first concrete example of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351199,
    "dDurationMs": 7521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how this could work in detail with his AdS/CFT\ncorrespondence. I talked in a lot of detail"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358720,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the nature of the AdS/CFT proposal in\nour previous episode—including the fact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365250,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AdS/CFT is valid for a negatively curved universe\nwith a negative cosmological constant—so-called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369210,
    "dDurationMs": 6790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anti-de Sitter or AdS space—quite different\nto our own de Sitter universe with its positive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376000,
    "dDurationMs": 1550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmological constant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377550,
    "dDurationMs": 7860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But AdS/CFT is still a powerful tool for exploring\nholographic emergence. And we’re going to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385410,
    "dDurationMs": 7690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "use just one part of this proposal—the CFT\npart. CFT is for conformal field theory. It’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393100,
    "dDurationMs": 7789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the laws of physics that play out on the boundary—that encode the universe in the bulk. A"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400889,
    "dDurationMs": 5191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conformal field theory is one in which the\nlaws governing the field theory don’t care"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406080,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the size-scale of the objects in the\ntheory. So you could have little wiggles in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411430,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the field that interact with each other in\na particular way, or gigantic wiggles that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416770,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interact in exactly the same way—as long\nas the wiggles are the same shape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421710,
    "dDurationMs": 6889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This conformal-ness gives us a way for the\nbulk to emerge from the boundary. Imagine"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428599,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the boundary consists of many Planck-length\nsquares, each able to hold a single bit—0"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434599,
    "dDurationMs": 6910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or 1—empty or full. You can have small-scale\npatterns of these bits, where each square"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441509,
    "dDurationMs": 6681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is independent. Or you could have larger-scale\npatterns that average over many squares—they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448190,
    "dDurationMs": 6409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are, as we say, course-grained. Because it’s\na conformal field theory these large patterns"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454599,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experience the same physics as the smaller\npatterns. And these patterns could appear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459789,
    "dDurationMs": 3371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on many scales, all behaving basically the\nsame."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463160,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And let’s add another fact—a system of\npatterns on one scale only interacts with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469240,
    "dDurationMs": 4919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patterns of the same scale. They don’t interact\nwith patterns on larger or smaller scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474159,
    "dDurationMs": 5331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s like having multiple sizes of ripples\non a pond that just wash over or under each"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479490,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480490,
    "dDurationMs": 5848,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there is something that’s different for the course-grained patterns vs the fine patterns —the amount"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486338,
    "dDurationMs": 5742,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of information needed to represent them over\nthe surface is lower. You’re effectively"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492080,
    "dDurationMs": 5649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lumping the Planck-length squares together.\nLet’s say we’re looking at just the patterns"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497729,
    "dDurationMs": 7250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that have twice the scale-length of the smallest-scale\npatterns. We can represent them using averaged"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504979,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "square sizes that each contain 4 of our original\nPlanck-length squares. We can also imagine"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511069,
    "dDurationMs": 6861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a surface containing only this pattern scale—it\nhas ¼ of the number of effective squares"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517930,
    "dDurationMs": 6279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as our original surface. That means we can\nalso think of it as having an effective radius"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524209,
    "dDurationMs": 3021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of ½ that of the original."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527230,
    "dDurationMs": 9635,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The larger the pattern size, the more Planck squares you fit into the effective pixel scale of that pattern size, and that means "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536865,
    "dDurationMs": 6385,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the smaller the effective radius of that scale will be. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543250,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So here's another way to represent this 2-D surface with its many pattern scales—and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548880,
    "dDurationMs": 7449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a series of nested spheres,\neach sphere representing one of those scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556329,
    "dDurationMs": 5071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The spheres near the center represent the\nlargest scales on the boundary where the patterns"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561400,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are so big they have relatively few effective\npixels to play with. That’s what makes the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567060,
    "dDurationMs": 6230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inner spheres effectively “small”, while\nthe outer spheres are occupied by the boundary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573290,
    "dDurationMs": 6750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patterns that are finer or smaller in scale—we need higher\nresolution to represent them, so these spheres"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580040,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are made of many effective pixels, and so are effectively large."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583940,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But because the boundary theory is a conformal\nfield theory, the physics on each sphere is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588890,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exactly the same. And if the separation between\nthese spheres is small enough—for example,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594170,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if it's also a Planck length—then we’ve\nproduced a 3-D space out of a 2-D one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600200,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a fairly cartoonish sketch of how\na non-spatial degree of freedom in a lower"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605480,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dimensional space can\nlead to an emergent space with one more dimension."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611610,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The real picture is likely to be way more\ncomplicated—for example, the emergence may"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616240,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not come from simple scale invariance but\nrather from quantum entanglement across many"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621880,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scales—and that’s something we’ll come\nback to another time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625620,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the story I just told is still a useful\npicture to get the basic gist of what a holographically"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631060,
    "dDurationMs": 6820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emergent space looks like. Because this new\ndegree of freedom translates to a radial direction,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637880,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that emergent space is in a sense “inside”\nthe original space. That’s why we can talk"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644180,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the higher dimensional space—the bulk—emerging\nfrom its boundary. Although really the bulk"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650480,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and boundary are two distinct spacetimes.\nIt may be that each completely contains all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656050,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the information of the other and the two\nevolve over time in lock-step with each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662290,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But they evolve by very different laws of\nphysics. There’s a boundary theory and a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667140,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bulk theory and on the surface they look like\nthey shouldn’t predict the same thing, yet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672350,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they do; they both predict the same universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677000,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is actually a recurring thing in physics.\nThere are a number of places where two wildly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682580,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different mathematical descriptions describe\nthe same system or phenomenon—and often"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687940,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the different descriptions seem mutually incompatible.\nThese are called dualities, and to me they’re"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694660,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of the most mysterious features of reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now for a true duality, there is no preferred side—neither of the two theories are more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704160,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fundamental than the other. For example, in\nquantum mechanics you can represent a particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709070,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a sum of waves in momentum space OR in\nposition space. Those are equally true, so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715570,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is a true duality. So is the holographic\nduality a true one? If so then the boundary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722290,
    "dDurationMs": 6270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the bulk are equivalently real—it wouldn’t\nbe quite right to say the interior emerges"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728560,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the boundary. Instead there’s a so-called\nontological democracy. Perhaps both emerge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735240,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from something more fundamental, but neither\nis primary to the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738910,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, there are also such things\nas approximate dualities, in which one side"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744880,
    "dDurationMs": 6990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really is more fundamental—one system “causes”\nthe other system in a meaningful sense. Now if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751870,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the holographic duality is like this then\nit’s much more reasonable to describe one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756800,
    "dDurationMs": 6860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sidie as emergent—and the emergent side\nin this case is most likely the bulk—but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763660,
    "dDurationMs": 6419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only because in the most concrete version\nof a holographic duality—AdS/CFT—the boundary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770079,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory is currently better defined than the\nbulk theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774149,
    "dDurationMs": 7031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So which is the case? Well even if our universe\npossesses some sort of holographic duality,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781180,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we don’t know whether it’d be a true or\napproximate duality. In fact, we don’t even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787180,
    "dDurationMs": 5409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know which type the AdS/CFT duality is. That’s\nsomething we’re going to have to spend a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792589,
    "dDurationMs": 5901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lot more time on—and I mean this show and\nthe theoretical physicists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798490,
    "dDurationMs": 6730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, so we’re up to speed on the holographic\nemergence of space. In upcoming episodes we’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805220,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see how gravity can enter the picture—first\nthrough the entropic gravity proposal of Eric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810900,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Verlinde. We’ll also see the modern thinking\nfor the emergence of space through quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816910,
    "dDurationMs": 5239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entanglement, and what the prospects are for\nfinding a concrete holographic description"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822149,
    "dDurationMs": 7681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for our universe. As a teaser, the holographic\nboundary may not even be at infinite distance,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829830,
    "dDurationMs": 6249,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but rather in the far future. And we’ll\nultimately get to the big question: is our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836079,
    "dDurationMs": 6441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe really emergent from a more fundamental,\nlower dimensional reality? Or are we just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842520,
    "dDurationMs": 6179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one side of the coin of a true duality—the\nboundary and the bulk—each an equally real"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848699,
    "dDurationMs": 2021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and mutually emergent spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850720,
    "dDurationMs": 8830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. The next Total Solar\nEclipse to hit North America is coming up on April 8th. In celebration"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859550,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this celestial event, we have some limited\ntime only space time merch. For those of you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864660,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who will be witnessing the eclipse, we have\na specialty UV glow print Solar Eclipse T-Shirt"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870470,
    "dDurationMs": 6059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u0026 Sweatshirt. Designed by our gfx team for\nthe eclipse, both the t-shirt and sweatshirt"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876529,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are UV charged, so after being out in the\nsun they’ll begin to glow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881779,
    "dDurationMs": 4841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The glasses, t-shirt \u0026 sweatshirt are all\nlimited only prints, so when we’re out of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886620,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stock we’re completely out! And if you want\nto ensure you get your glasses and shirt before"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892210,
    "dDurationMs": 7730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the eclipse you have put your order in by\nMarch 26th. So head over pbsspacetime.com/shop"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899940,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or click the link in the description, to put\nyour order in now."
    } ]
  } ]
}
